=> Theory

set -o is used to view or enable shell options.

First, try:

set -o

It displays many shell options, for example:

allexport       off
braceexpand     on
errexit         off
noglob          off
nounset         off
pipefail        off
xtrace          off

The exact list can vary depending on the shell.

=> You can also use:

set -o xtrace

This is another way of enabling debugging.

It is equivalent to:

set -x

=> Similarly:

set -o errexit

is equivalent to:

set -e

=> Important: set -o itself vs set -o option

There are two different things to understand.

a) set -o

Shows the current shell options and whether they are on/off.

b) set -o option

For example:

set -o xtrace

Enables a particular option.

=> Simple Lab

Create one script:

nano options.sh

Add:

#!/bin/bash

set -x
set -e

echo "Starting script"

name="Bilal"

echo "Name is $name"

ls /tmp

echo "Script completed"

Run:

chmod +x options.sh
./options.sh

You'll see the commands being displayed because of:

set -x

And if a command fails, set -e will stop the script.
